ve the same dimension as $x$","Because each coordinate of $x$ is paired with the corresponding coordinate of $y$","Because the last coordinate is always ignored"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f8e08f8bbf209170f7ceb0c4","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When can $x\\cdot x$ be equal to $0$ for a real vector $x$?","options":["Only when $x$ is the zero vector","Whenever the coordinates of $x$ add to $0$","For every unit vector","Whenever $x$ has a negative coordinate"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8e2b1c3b49e81c03b4e8e84d","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For vectors $x,y,z\\in\\mathbb R^n$, which expression equals $(x+z)\\cdot y$ by linearity of the dot product?","options":["$x\\cdot y$","$(x\\cdot y)(z\\cdot y)$","$x\\cdot z$","$x\\cdot y+z\\cdot y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0ecf72492c779a2696813b16","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_005","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For vectors $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, what upper bound does the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality give for $|x\\cdot y|$?","options":["$\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$","$\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$","$\\|x\\|^{2}\\|y\\|^{2}$","$\\tfrac12(\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9a2dd976ce6f599b99eeeb7e","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $(1,2)\\cdot(3,4)$.","options":["$7$","$14$","$10$","$11$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5614a30a23fa59689644848","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $(2,-1,3)\\cdot(0,5,2)$.","options":["$-1$","$7$","$1$","$11$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"808db268698920eb23f550eb","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair of vectors has dot product $0$?","options":["$(2,0)$ and $(1,0)$","$(1,1)$ and $(1,1)$","$(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$","$(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5cefdc16892df35a50b51d75","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(2,-3)$, what is $x\\cdot x$?","options":["$5$","$13$","$-1$","$25$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ccbe813ee3b11c0e4f83229","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Find $a$ if $(1,a)\\cdot(2,3)=0$.","options":["$\\frac23$","$3$","$-2$","$-\\frac23$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac4eaecf8f27d9211360ca32","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $x\\cdot y \\lt 0$, what does this usually say about the directions of nonzero vectors $x$ and $y$?","options":["They point more opposite than similar","They must be equal","They must both be zero","They must be perpendicular"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6f36a0d7e37d151dec4c6e9c","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For nonzero vectors $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, what can be computed from $x\\cdot y=\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|\\cos\\theta$?","options":["a determinant","the angle $\\theta$ between them","their sum $x+y$","a cross product"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c023994db09c86345f56c473","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $x\\cdot x$ never negative for real vectors?","options":["It is defined to be $1$ for every vector","The formula uses absolute values instead of products","It is a sum of squares of real coordinates","Negative coordinates are omitted from the formula"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c470b348d890419c7fbc140c","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $(1,-2,0)\\cdot(4,1,5)$.","options":["$9$","$2$","$0$","$-6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7e5eb5c7ff542c048139f35","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,2)$ and $y=(t,1)$, which value of $t$ makes $x$ orthogonal to $y$?","options":["$0$","$2$","$-2$","$-\\frac12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2720ae146f76a500077d0ac7","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $u\\cdot v=6$ for real vectors, what is $v\\cdot u$?","options":["It cannot be determined.","$-6$","$0$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d30bd104b1ed944012b7808","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does $x\\cdot x\\ge0$ not imply that $x\\cdot y\\ge0$ for all real vectors $x,y$?","options":["The formula for $x\\cdot y$ is not defined for real vectors","Different vectors can point in opposite directions","The dot product ignores signs of all coordinates","The vector $y$ is always equal to $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9ad7a6aab7a9750fd70e915c","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A nonzero vector has $x\\cdot y=0$ with another nonzero vector $y$. What should be concluded?","options":["The dot product formula has failed","The two vectors are orthogonal, not necessarily zero","The two vectors point in the same direction as each other","The two vectors are equal"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1672138ad68a0877dfae75f5","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch16_inner_product_019",